 and creating insecurity in previously stable areas. “Everything that had been agreed upon has been abandoned, most recently the provisional constitution,” Mr Deni said. He also referred to what he described as attacks involving drones against officials who had served the country, as well as the deployment of national security forces in ways he said were contributing to instability and the alleged seizure of public and private property. The claims were made as political tensions remain centred on Somalia’s electoral arrangements, constitutional changes and the relationship between the federal government and member states. Mr Deni said Puntland would not accept what he described as an election process designed exclusively by the federal government in Mogadishu. He called on Somalis and other stakeholders to convene a broad national conference aimed at resolving the political dispute and protecting the country’s federal system. “The Puntland government is not satisfied with the ambitions and the type of election announced by Hassan Sheikh, which we believe threatens the existence of the Somali Republic,” he said. Mr Deni said Puntland would communicate its position to both the Somali public and the international community. He also said he understood the concerns of Puntland residents and promised that his administration would not take decisions that he believed were contrary to their interests. The Puntland president’s remarks come amid continuing disagreements in Somalia over constitutional reform, electoral arrangements and the distribution of political authority between the federal government and Somalia’s federal member states. Explore more on these topics Puntland Somalia Related Posts Senior Turkish Delegation Visits Somalia as Bilateral Cooperation Expands 1 min read Somalia pledges support for Ugandan businesses as trade ties deepen 3 min read Somali Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre arrives in Jeddah 1 min read Somalia condemns deadly attack on church in Jordan