      "title": "Puntland President Warns Somalia Faces Threat to Statehood Amid Election Dispute - Garowe Online",
      "link": "https://www.garoweonline.com/en/news/somalia/puntland-president-warns-somalia-faces-threat-to-statehood-amid-election-dispute",
      "date": "Sun, 25 Oct 2026 12:35:44 GMT",
      "image": "https://uploads-garowe.s3.amazonaws.com/upload/images/Article/2026/1792931716/main/og.PNG",
      "content": "Puntland President Warns Somalia Faces Threat to Statehood Amid Election Dispute Latest Headlines Puntland President Warns Somalia Faces Threat to Statehood Amid Election Dispute U.S. Intelligence Warns Iranian Plots Against Americans in Middle East Have Advanced Thousands Flee Deadly Ethiopia Clashes as Moyale Violence Spills Into Kenya China and Djibouti Send Clear Message to Israel Over Somaliland Recognition Cameroon’s president accused of merely ‘visiting’ his nation after Swiss stays Advertise with us Home News Puntland Somalia World Sports Press Releases Somaliland Videos Opinion Contact Privacy Policy English Somali Puntland President Warns Somalia Faces Threat to Statehood Amid Election Dispute Staff Reporter GAROWE ONLINE Posted On 25-10-2026, 12:29PM GAROWE, Somalia — Puntland President Said Abdullahi Deni on Sunday accused Somalia’s term-ended federal government of undermining the country’s federal system and political agreements, calling for a national conference to resolve disputes over elections and constitutional amendments. Speaking at the opening of the 59th session of Puntland’s parliament, Deni said Somalia had entered what he described as a dangerous period for statehood, blaming President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud’s administration for failing to honour agreements underpinning the country’s federal system. “Puntland has always stood for building a federal Somali state, and we have invested every kind of effort to achieve stability and establish complete systems of governance,” Deni said. “I deeply regret that the country has now entered a situation that threatens the Somali statehood that has been built over a long period.” Deni said previous political agreements had been abandoned, including Somalia’s provisional constitution, which he said had provided a framework for political cooperation. He accused Hassan of refusing to organise elections based on an agreement among political stakeholders and of obstructing dialogue intended to resolve the country’s political disputes. The Puntland leader also alleged that political instability had escalated to the point where officials who had served the country were being targeted by drone attacks, while peaceful areas were being destabilised. He further accused the federal government of using national security forces to damage the country and seize public and private property. The allegations were not independently verified. “Everything that had been agreed upon has fallen away, most recently the 2012 provisional constitution,” Deni said, referring to the framework that has guided Somalia’s federal political system. Deni, who has repeatedly clashed with the federal government over constitutional changes and electoral arrangements, said Puntland would not accept what he described as an election process designed exclusively by Villa Somalia, the seat of the federal presidency in Mogadishu. The dispute comes amid broader political tensions over constitutional amendments, the organisation of future elections and the division of powers between Somalia’s federal government and its member states. Puntland has argued that major constitutional and electoral decisions require broad political agreement, while disagreements over the process have strained relations between Garowe and Mogadishu. Deni called on Somalis concerned about the country’s future to convene an urgent national conference aimed at safeguarding state institutions and reaching agreement on the way forward. He said Puntland would also consult the Somali public and the international community over its objections to the federal government’s electoral plans. “I fully understand the feelings of the people of Puntland, and I promise you that I will not make decisions that are unworthy of you,” Deni told lawmakers. He said Hassan and those who had supported his decisions would bear responsibility for the consequences of the current political situation. The federal government’s response to Deni’s latest remarks was not included in the statement. Somalia has struggled for years to establish an agreed constitutional framework and a stable electoral system following decades of conflict and political fragmentation. Disagreements between the federal government and regional administrations remain a major challenge to political consensus and the development of democratic institutions. GAROWE ONLINE Latest Headlines U.S. Intelligence Warns Iranian Plots Against Americans in Middle East Have Advanced 25-10-2026, 05:53AM Thousands Flee Deadly Ethiopia Clashes as Moyale Violence Spills Into Kenya 25-10-2026, 05:05AM China and Djibouti Send Clear Message to Israel Over Somaliland Recognition 24-10-2026, 05:51AM Cameroon’s president accused of merely ‘visiting’ his nation after Swiss stays 23-10-2026, 08:25AM Advertise with us Advertise with GaroweOnline and expose your brand to a global audience of loyal visitors from across the world $50 $25 $10 $5 Other $ ONE TIME MONTHLY DONATE GET IN TOUCH Latest Videos Jubaland president Ahmed Madobe sets conditions on election Videos 29-11-2020 01:12AM Federal Interior minister speaks about next eelctions Videos 16-11-2020 01:31PM Somaliland suspends engagement with UN humanitarian agencies Videos 01-11-2020 05:26PM Child traffickers arrested in Somalia Videos 23-10-2020 10:52PM Related Articles Hormuud Telecom hosts fifth Somali Success Story Forum focused on healthcare sector He also conveyed congratulations from Senate Speaker Abdi Hashi Abdullahi to Somalia’s Success Story Forum (BGS26). Somalia 21-10-2026 09:30AM Somalia: Puntland Health Minister Inspects Dhahar Hospital During Regional Tour He later visited Dhahar District Hospital and several other health facilities to assess their operations and the medical services available to residents. Somalia 21-10-2026 04:40AM Somalia’s Opposition Alliance to Hold Key Political Talks in Nairobi, Kenya Somalia 19-10-2026 09:27AM Fahad Yasin Accuses Hassan Sheikh of Leading Somalia Toward Two Governments and Two Armies Somalia 16-10-2026 10:23PM Somalia : Hassan Sheikh Visits Turkish Vessel in Move Seen as Public Diversion Somalia 16-10-2026 07:51PM Somalia: Laftagareen’s Forces Expand Territorial Gains in Southwest as Buurhakaba Falls Somalia 15-10-2026 05:39PM Home News Videos Gallery Opinion About Us Contact Privacy Policy Copyright © GAROWONLINE All Rights Reserved Advertise with us Advertise with GaroweOnline and expose your brand to a global audience of loyal visitors from across the world $50 $25 $10 $5 Other $ ONE TIME MONTHLY DONATE GET IN TOUCH Are you happy to accept cookies? To manage your cookie choices now, including how to opt out where our partners rely on legitimate interests to use your information. Yes, I’m happy",
      "summary": "Puntland President Said Abdullahi Deni has issued a stark warning regarding the threat to Somalia's statehood amid ongoing election disputes. Speaking at the opening of Puntland's parliamentary session, Deni accused the federal government, led by President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud, of undermining the federal system and failing to uphold political agreements, including the provisional constitution. He expressed concern that the country is entering a perilous phase, with political instability escalating to violence, including drone attacks targeting officials. Deni emphasized that Puntland will not accept an election process dictated solely by the federal government and called for a national conference to address these issues. He urged Somalis to unite in safeguarding state institutions and stressed the need for broad political consensus on constitutional and electoral matters. The Puntland leader's remarks highlight the ongoing tensions between regional administrations and the federal government, complicating efforts to establish a stable political framework in Somalia.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/puntland-president-warns-somalia-faces-threat-to-statehood-a-7ed506909c.html"
